        "geology": "The prevalent bedrock in the national park Hunsrück-Hochwald is quartzite. At some locations the colour of the quartzite is white and grey. But at certain locations the colour turns into red because of iron in the soil. The quartzite can be seen at several places above the ground as great rocks. These rocks offer specific habitats for reptiles.",
        "hydrology": "The national park comprises several wet locations like moors. A special kind of moor, the hanging bog, can be found there, too. There is a special programme ongoing, which intends to remove the spruces out of the moors to restore them to their natural state and to activate their ability to store water and carbon dioxide. Furthermore, there are many springs and brooks in the national park.",
        "soils": "The prevalent type of soil in the national park is gley. At some locations the ground can be very marshy, e.g. in the moors. ",
        "vegetation": "The national park is a mixed forest containing mainly beeches and spruces. Occasionally, douglas firs can be seen, too. Mosses and lichens are part of the vegetation as well as meadows of arnica. "
      },
